Found during k126 (worker). §3 "Merging sources" reads as if fields merged freely (project < user < local), but every source entry needs its own backend field at load, so a skilletor.local.json cannot say only { "path": [...] } or { "ref": "x" } for a source the project declares. Decide: allow a backend-less entry when another file supplies the backend, or say in §3 and the README that an overriding entry repeats the backend.

2026-10-15 02:01 Maintainer decision 2026-10-15: allow a backend-less entry when another config file supplies the backend for that source (ref/path override alone); an entry with no backend anywhere stays an error.

2026-10-15 04:07 Rule taken: backend fields keep merging per field (k66) — a higher git/url/local replaces only the same field and sits beside the others, so a user local over a project git stays author mode with the git as fallback (checked: the real ~/.claude/skilletor.json + this repo still load unchanged). ref/path: last file giving one wins. ref is the git backend field: used only when git is chosen; it stays when a higher file gives another git without ref (a pin is never dropped silently, k88 spirit; same-repo SSH/HTTPS re-declarations keep their pin); a higher ref replaces it. Backend-less entry: allowed in any file when another file of the merge gives one; none anywhere = ConfigError "<files joined by and>: sources.X needs one of git, url or local; no other config file gives one / none of these files gives one (...)". User scope reads the user config alone, so a backend-less user entry is dropped from userSources; a user install/bundle naming it is a ConfigError saying why. With no project scope the user config alone must give the backend.

2026-10-15 04:07 Also found (not fixed, for this board): `source remove` of the one entry giving the backend (e.g. project git) while skilletor.local.json still has {ref} for that name leaves a config load refuses; the command edits, then its sync fails with the new clear ConfigError. Could pre-check and refuse/name the local file. Also: there is no way for a higher file to unset a lower ref (unpin) — only to replace it.

